\title{Drag on Smooth Circular Cylinders Towed Through a Water Tank}
\author{\textit{[Authors and affiliations to be added]}}
\date{\textit{[Submission date]}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Drag was measured for smooth circular cylinders towed through a water tank at nominal speeds from \SI{0.2}{m.s^{-1}} to \SI{1.1}{m.s^{-1}}. Three cylinder diameters (\SI{25.4}{mm}, \SI{38.1}{mm}, and \SI{50.8}{mm}) were tested, each with a nominal submerged length of \SI{0.60}{m}. Mean force was obtained from the steady portion of each load-cell record after tare subtraction and calibration. Across the 30 available runs, the calculated drag coefficients were approximately 1.14, 1.15, and 1.18 for the 1-in, 1.5-in, and 2-in cylinders, respectively. The present records span Reynolds numbers of order $5\times10^3$ to $5.6\times10^4$ when evaluated using the existing analysis convention. The measurements show broadly similar coefficients across the tested sizes, but interpretation is limited by an unresolved high value in one 2-in run, a calibration remount between test campaigns, uncorrected temperature and blockage effects, and the absence of reported uncertainty and repeat runs. These results are therefore preliminary pending a consistent reanalysis.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} cylinder drag; towing tank; force measurement; Reynolds number

\section{Introduction}
The drag on a circular cylinder is commonly expressed through a dimensionless coefficient that depends on Reynolds number and the flow and boundary conditions. A towing tank provides a direct way to measure the force on a stationary test article relative to the surrounding water. The present work records drag on three smooth circular cylinders over a range of tow speeds, with the goal of comparing the resulting coefficients across diameter and Reynolds number.

This manuscript is a working draft. The project files do not yet contain a literature review or citations, so the broader context and comparison with published cylinder-drag data must be added before submission. The precise test-article geometry, surface finish, mounting arrangement, and facility details also require confirmation.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Facility and test articles}
Tests were conducted in a water tank reported in the project notes as \SI{0.9}{m} wide and \SI{0.75}{m} deep. Smooth circular cylinders of nominal diameters \SI{25.4}{mm} (1 in), \SI{38.1}{mm} (1.5 in), and \SI{50.8}{mm} (2 in) were towed with a nominal submerged length $L=\SI{0.60}{m}$. More detail on the towing mechanism, support, cylinder end conditions, clearance from the tank boundaries, and surface finish was not present in the project record.

The 1-in tests were recorded on 14 October 2025 and the 2-in tests on 3 November 2025. The 1.5-in tests were recorded on 12 March 2026. Ten nominal speeds from \SI{0.2}{m.s^{-1}} to \SI{1.1}{m.s^{-1}} in \SI{0.1}{m.s^{-1}} increments were logged for each cylinder. Logged water temperatures ranged from \SI{16.9}{\degreeCelsius} to \SI{17.6}{\degreeCelsius} for the 1-in series, \SI{15.0}{\degreeCelsius} to \SI{15.6}{\degreeCelsius} for the 2-in series, and \SI{13.7}{\degreeCelsius} to \SI{14.3}{\degreeCelsius} for the 1.5-in series.

\subsection{Force acquisition and processing}
Load-cell voltage was recorded at \SI{200}{Hz}. For the October and November campaigns, sensitivity was estimated by a linear fit to the September 2025 hanging-mass calibration data; the processing script reports approximately \SI{0.020}{V.N^{-1}}. The March campaign followed a load-cell remount and used its March calibration, with a sensitivity of \SI{0.0213}{V.N^{-1}}. The March data used a semicolon-delimited format and were processed separately.

For each run, the mean voltage over the first second was used as the tare. Voltage was converted to force according to
\begin{equation}
  F(t) = \frac{V(t)-\overline{V}_{t<1\,\mathrm{s}}}{S},
\end{equation}
where $S$ is the campaign sensitivity in volts per newton. The mean force and sample standard deviation were calculated over $3<t<8$ s. The existing reduction uses the nominal speed and assumes a constant submerged length of \SI{0.60}{m}.

\subsection{Dimensionless quantities}
The analysis notebook calculated Reynolds number and drag coefficient as
\begin{equation}
  Re = \frac{U D}{\nu}, \qquad
  C_D = \frac{\overline{F}}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho U^2 D L},
\end{equation}
using $\rho=\SI{998}{kg.m^{-3}}$ and $\nu=\SI{1.004e-6}{m^2.s^{-1}}$ (water properties at \SI{20}{\degreeCelsius} in the current notebook). The force normalization uses projected frontal area $DL$. This convention is retained here to reproduce the existing analysis; the reference-area definition should be checked against the intended literature convention before publication.

\section{Results}
All 30 logged runs have corresponding processed force values. Based on the current notebook calculation, the mean drag coefficients for each diameter are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:summary}. The standard deviations shown are across the ten speed points and describe observed spread, not measurement uncertainty.

