\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:cdre} shows the run-level coefficients against Reynolds number. The coefficients are of similar magnitude among the three cylinders over the tested range. The 2-in series has the largest spread, including run 17 at $C_D\approx1.357$. The run log notes that the zero appeared anomalous after this run, so this point should be reviewed against its raw trace and calibration history before it is included in a final estimate.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{../figures/cd_vs_re.png}
\caption{Drag coefficient against Reynolds number from the existing analysis notebook. Values use $\nu=1.004\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$ for every campaign and have not been corrected for blockage.}
\label{fig:cdre}
\end{figure}

As an example of the force record, run 5 (1-in cylinder, nominal speed \SI{0.6}{m.s^{-1}}) reaches a sustained force plateau after the start-up transient. The processing interval $3<t<8$ s lies within this plateau (Figure~\ref{fig:trace}).

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{../figures/timeseries_run005.png}
\caption{Processed force time series for run 5. Shading marks the $3$--$8$ s interval used to calculate mean drag.}
\label{fig:trace}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion}
The current reduction gives mean coefficients between 1.14 and 1.18 for the three diameters. Within this preliminary dataset, there is no clear separation by cylinder diameter, although the measurements are not sufficient to establish a universal coefficient or a Reynolds-number trend. In particular, each speed appears to have only one run per cylinder, so the scatter across speed combines any physical variation with run-to-run and instrument effects.

Several corrections and checks are needed before drawing a quantitative comparison. First, the water temperature differed between campaigns, while the notebook used a single \SI{20}{\degreeCelsius} kinematic viscosity. Temperature-specific viscosity will shift the Reynolds numbers, especially for the colder March data. Second, the cylinders occupy a non-negligible fraction of the tank dimensions; the notes identify blockage correction as an open issue. No correction has been applied. Third, the March series used a remounted load cell and a different calibration, so campaign-level calibration uncertainty and any tare drift should be assessed. Finally, run 17 has a documented zero concern and should be inspected or excluded under a stated rule, with results reported both with and without it if warranted.

The reported force standard deviations in the processed files quantify fluctuations within the selected time window. They do not include uncertainty in calibration, tow speed, cylinder dimensions, submerged length, water properties, alignment, or blockage. A complete uncertainty budget and repeated runs are needed to support formal error bars.

\section{Conclusions}
Drag measurements were collected for 1-in, 1.5-in, and 2-in smooth circular cylinders at ten nominal speeds between \SI{0.2}{m.s^{-1}} and \SI{1.1}{m.s^{-1}}. Applying the current force processing and coefficient definition gives mean $C_D$ values of 1.144, 1.153, and 1.178, respectively. These values provide a useful preliminary summary, but should not be treated as final calibrated results until water-property, blockage, calibration, and run-17 checks are completed.

\section*{Data and code availability}
Raw measurements, calibration records, the run log, processing scripts, and the analysis notebook are included with this project. The manuscript values above were transcribed from the processed force files using the assumptions in the notebook. A reproducible manuscript build and a unified analysis pipeline remain to be added.

